1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,205
Three expressions that look nothing alike.

2
00:00:03,205 --> 00:00:05,442
Sine squared plus cosine squared.

3
00:00:05,442 --> 00:00:07,679
Secant squared minus tan squared.

4
00:00:07,679 --> 00:00:10,017
Cosecant squared minus cot squared.

5
00:00:10,417 --> 00:00:15,136
Put in any angle: seventeen, fifty,
one hundred and twenty three degrees.

6
00:00:15,586 --> 00:00:18,592
Aarav tried this on his
calculator last night.

7
00:00:18,942 --> 00:00:21,627
Every time, all three give exactly one.

8
00:00:21,627 --> 00:00:22,950
Luck, or a law?

9
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:24,567
It is a law.

10
00:00:24,567 --> 00:00:29,028
These are trigonometric identities,
and one circle proves all three.

11
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:31,502
First, what is an identity?

12
00:00:31,852 --> 00:00:36,701
An equation like two x equals six
is true for one value only: x equals

13
00:00:36,701 --> 00:00:37,601
three.

14
00:00:37,751 --> 00:00:42,252
An identity is true for every angle,
wherever both sides are defined.

15
00:00:42,652 --> 00:00:45,060
So one lucky angle proves nothing.

16
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:47,033
Pause and think.

17
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:50,871
Sine theta equals cosine
theta at forty five degrees.

18
00:00:50,871 --> 00:00:52,423
Is it an identity?

19
00:00:55,623 --> 00:00:56,523
No!

20
00:00:56,113 --> 00:00:59,684
At zero degrees sine is
zero, but cosine is one.

21
00:00:59,684 --> 00:01:01,763
One failing angle breaks it.

22
00:01:02,413 --> 00:01:06,175
Now the unit circle: radius
one, centre at the origin.

23
00:01:06,525 --> 00:01:09,806
Turn a radius through an
angle theta, to a point P.

24
00:01:10,206 --> 00:01:15,132
The x coordinate of P is cosine
theta, the y coordinate sine theta.

25
00:01:15,532 --> 00:01:16,965
Watch theta count up.

26
00:01:16,965 --> 00:01:19,737
The blue base and coral
height keep changing.

27
00:01:20,137 --> 00:01:23,806
But the slanted side is the
radius: always exactly one.

28
00:01:24,406 --> 00:01:26,588
That one fact is the whole proof.

29
00:01:26,938 --> 00:01:27,838
Step one.

30
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:30,433
Drop a line from P to the x axis.

31
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:34,785
The right triangle has base cosine
theta and height sine theta.

32
00:01:35,535 --> 00:01:36,435
Step two.

33
00:01:36,371 --> 00:01:39,940
The hypotenuse is the
radius, so its length is one.

34
00:01:40,690 --> 00:01:41,604
Step three.

35
00:01:41,604 --> 00:01:46,018
Pythagoras: base squared plus height
squared equals hypotenuse squared.

36
00:01:46,018 --> 00:01:48,732
The two small squares match the big one.

37
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:50,439
Step four.

38
00:01:50,439 --> 00:01:54,616
So cosine squared theta plus
sine squared theta equals one.

39
00:01:55,366 --> 00:01:58,220
Sine squared theta means
sine theta, squared.

40
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:00,999
The square is on the sine, not the angle.

41
00:02:01,399 --> 00:02:03,523
Slide theta all the way round.

42
00:02:03,523 --> 00:02:08,426
Cosine turns negative in the second
quadrant, but its square stays positive.

43
00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:09,926
The total stays one.

44
00:02:10,576 --> 00:02:13,090
Now the second identity, from the first.

45
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:18,518
Divide every term by cosine squared
theta, allowed only when cosine theta

46
00:02:18,518 --> 00:02:19,675
is not zero.

47
00:02:20,425 --> 00:02:23,926
Sine squared over cosine
squared is tan squared theta.

48
00:02:24,676 --> 00:02:26,888
Cosine squared over itself is one.

49
00:02:27,638 --> 00:02:30,750
One over cosine squared
is secant squared theta.

50
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:35,354
So, one plus tan squared theta
equals secant squared theta.

51
00:02:36,004 --> 00:02:36,921
Picture it.

52
00:02:36,921 --> 00:02:39,791
Stretch the triangle
until its base is one.

53
00:02:40,191 --> 00:02:45,092
Its height becomes tan theta, and its
slanted side becomes secant theta.

54
00:02:45,092 --> 00:02:46,320
Same Pythagoras.

55
00:02:46,970 --> 00:02:51,729
The third identity: divide by sine
squared theta, where sine theta is not

56
00:02:51,729 --> 00:02:52,629
zero.

57
00:02:53,048 --> 00:02:57,298
We get one plus cot squared theta
equals cosecant squared theta, with the

58
00:02:57,298 --> 00:02:58,866
height stretched to one.

59
00:02:59,516 --> 00:03:00,677
Side by side.

60
00:03:00,677 --> 00:03:04,255
Identity one works for
every angle, no exceptions.

61
00:03:04,655 --> 00:03:07,425
Identity two needs a non zero cosine.

62
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:12,240
At ninety degrees cosine is zero,
so tan and secant do not exist.

63
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:13,809
Quick check.

64
00:03:13,809 --> 00:03:18,335
At ninety degrees, is one plus tan
squared theta equal to secant squared

65
00:03:18,335 --> 00:03:19,235
theta?

66
00:03:18,945 --> 00:03:20,173
Pause and think.

67
00:03:23,373 --> 00:03:24,273
No!

68
00:03:23,901 --> 00:03:27,135
At ninety degrees both
sides are undefined.

69
00:03:27,135 --> 00:03:31,367
An identity only promises
equality where both sides exist.

70
00:03:32,017 --> 00:03:33,125
Remember this.

71
00:03:33,125 --> 00:03:36,293
Three identities, one
circle, one Pythagoras.

72
00:03:36,993 --> 00:03:38,728
Now, one worked example.

73
00:03:39,078 --> 00:03:43,505
Secant theta is thirteen fifths,
with theta in the fourth quadrant.

74
00:03:43,505 --> 00:03:46,682
Find tan theta, sine
theta and cosecant theta.

75
00:03:47,182 --> 00:03:48,082
Step one.

76
00:03:48,034 --> 00:03:52,680
By the second identity, tan squared
theta is secant squared minus one:

77
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:55,776
one sixty nine over
twenty five, minus one.

78
00:03:55,776 --> 00:03:58,613
That is one forty four over twenty five.

79
00:03:59,363 --> 00:04:00,263
Step two.

80
00:04:00,207 --> 00:04:03,283
So tan theta is plus
or minus twelve fifths.

81
00:04:03,283 --> 00:04:07,892
In the fourth quadrant y is negative,
so it is minus twelve fifths.

82
00:04:08,642 --> 00:04:09,572
Step three.

83
00:04:09,572 --> 00:04:12,938
Sine is tan over secant:
minus twelve thirteenths.

84
00:04:13,688 --> 00:04:14,627
Step four.

85
00:04:14,627 --> 00:04:18,273
Cosecant is one over sine:
minus thirteen twelfths.

86
00:04:19,023 --> 00:04:23,714
Check: one plus twenty five over one
forty four is one sixty nine over

87
00:04:23,714 --> 00:04:26,118
one forty four, cosecant squared.

88
00:04:26,768 --> 00:04:28,233
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:04:28,583 --> 00:04:33,325
Many students square sine theta plus
cosine theta, and write sine squared

90
00:04:33,325 --> 00:04:35,854
plus cosine squared, which is one.

91
00:04:36,254 --> 00:04:40,657
But a bracket squared has a middle
term: one plus two sine theta cosine

92
00:04:40,657 --> 00:04:41,557
theta.

93
00:04:41,248 --> 00:04:43,450
At forty five degrees it is two.

94
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:45,725
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:45,725 --> 00:04:50,274
JEE Main and the PUC boards:
given one ratio, find the rest.

96
00:04:50,874 --> 00:04:52,215
Exam level now.

97
00:04:52,215 --> 00:04:55,307
Secant theta plus tan theta equals three.

98
00:04:55,307 --> 00:04:56,705
Find sine theta.

99
00:04:56,705 --> 00:04:58,104
Pause and solve.

100
00:05:03,504 --> 00:05:04,935
The answer is B.

101
00:05:04,935 --> 00:05:06,012
Four fifths.

102
00:05:06,512 --> 00:05:11,244
Secant squared minus tan squared is
one, which is secant minus tan, times

103
00:05:11,244 --> 00:05:12,487
secant plus tan.

104
00:05:12,987 --> 00:05:15,880
So secant minus tan equals one third.

105
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:19,195
Add the equations: secant is five thirds.

106
00:05:19,195 --> 00:05:21,291
Subtract: tan is four thirds.

107
00:05:21,891 --> 00:05:23,701
Sine is tan over secant.

108
00:05:23,701 --> 00:05:26,732
Four thirds over five
thirds is four fifths.

109
00:05:27,332 --> 00:05:28,793
Here is your recap.

110
00:05:28,793 --> 00:05:30,294
Screenshot this card.

111
00:05:33,494 --> 00:05:35,695
Can you answer these three without help?

112
00:05:36,095 --> 00:05:36,995
One.

113
00:05:36,673 --> 00:05:40,698
Why is sine theta equals
cosine theta not an identity?

114
00:05:41,598 --> 00:05:42,498
Two.

115
00:05:42,131 --> 00:05:46,115
If cosine theta is three
fifths, what can sine theta be?

116
00:05:47,015 --> 00:05:47,915
Three.

117
00:05:47,679 --> 00:05:51,262
At which angles is the
third identity not defined?

118
00:05:52,362 --> 00:05:57,266
Next time: is sine of thirty plus
sixty equal to sine thirty plus sine

119
00:05:57,266 --> 00:05:58,166
sixty?

120
00:05:57,932 --> 00:05:59,210
Compound angles.
